        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Are manual treadmills any good?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              They are good at specific jobs. A flat manual is a cheap, outlet-free way to walk uphill indoors and fold the machine away. A curved manual is an excellent sprint and conditioning tool. Neither is a good general-purpose treadmill: flat ones are too short and stiff for running, and curved ones are expensive and hard work at easy walking paces.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Why is my manual treadmill so hard to push?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Mostly friction between the belt and deck, which a motor would normally overcome for you, plus flywheel inertia and any magnetic resistance. The slope is what helps drive the belt, so the shallowest incline setting is the stiffest. Try the steepest setting and lowest resistance, check the belt tension and alignment, and wear shoes with grip.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can you run on a manual treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              On a curved manual treadmill, yes — they are built for running and sprinting. On a flat manual, not in any sustained way. The belts are about 41 to 42 inches long and 12 to 13 inches wide, the frames weigh around 50 pounds, and the belt resists each push. Short jogging bursts are possible; a 30-minute run is not realistic.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can you set a flat manual treadmill to level?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              No. Flat manual treadmills rely on a fixed slope, usually between 5 and 10 degrees, to let your feet drive the belt. Without it the belt would barely move. Some offer two or three steep settings. If you want to walk on a level surface or vary incline from zero, you need a motorised treadmill.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Do manual treadmills burn more calories?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              At the same speed, a flat manual usually demands more effort because you are climbing a fixed incline and pushing the belt. A curved trainer typically feels harder than a motorised belt at the same pace too. Console calorie figures on all treadmills are estimates, though, and the effort you can sustain matters more than the machine.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a curved treadmill worth the extra money over a flat manual?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Only if you will run on it. A curved trainer costs roughly eight to twelve times as much as a flat manual and is a far better machine for sprints and intervals. If you mostly want to walk, the extra money buys capability you will not use, and a flat manual or a motorised walking machine is the better purchase.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <!-- Why trust us -->
        <section id="why-trust-us" class="ed-trust not-prose">
          <h2>Why trust Treadmill Reviews USA?</h2>
          <p>
